en: '<p class="l-text"><strong>Programs become truly powerful when they can make decisions and repeat actions.</strong> In Lesson 1, you learned variables and data types — the raw materials. Now you will learn <em>control flow</em> (how your program decides what to do) and <em>functions</em> (how you organize reusable logic). These two concepts together transform a flat script into a structured, intelligent program. Every ML pipeline, every data processing workflow, every API handler uses these building blocks thousands of times.</p>'

+ '<p class="l-text">Think of control flow like a train switching tracks: at each junction (if/elif/else), the program picks a path based on conditions. Functions are like stations — self-contained units that do a specific job. Master these, and you can build anything.</p>'
+ '<div class="lesson-outcomes" style="background:rgba(200,169,110,0.06);border-left:3px solid #c8a96e;padding:1rem 1.2rem;margin:1.5rem 0;border-radius:0 8px 8px 0"><div style="font-size:0.78rem;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:0.08em;color:#c8a96e;margin-bottom:0.6rem">📍 WHAT YOU\'LL LEARN</div><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:1.3rem;line-height:1.65;font-size:0.93rem;color:rgba(235,230,220,0.92)"><li>Control flow with if/elif/else, for, while, break, and continue</li><li>Iterate cleanly using enumerate, zip, range, and reversed</li><li>Define functions with positional, keyword, default, and *args/**kwargs parameters</li><li>Use lambda and map/filter idioms for short transformations</li><li>Catch and raise exceptions with try/except/finally and custom error classes</li></ul></div>'


/* ============================================================
   SECTION 1: Conditional Statements
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">1. Conditional Statements</h2>'

+ '<div class="calc-highlight"><strong>Core idea:</strong> Conditional statements let your program choose between different paths of execution based on Boolean conditions. Every <code>if</code> evaluates to <code>True</code> or <code>False</code> — and Python\'s indentation-based blocks make the structure visually clear.</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text">The fundamental pattern is <code>if / elif / else</code>. Python evaluates conditions top-to-bottom and executes the <strong>first</strong> block that matches. Once a block runs, the rest are skipped entirely:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code>score = <span class="num">85</span>\n\n<span class="kw">if</span> score >= <span class="num">90</span>:\n    grade = <span class="str">"A"</span>\n    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Excellent!"</span>)\n<span class="kw">elif</span> score >= <span class="num">80</span>:\n    grade = <span class="str">"B"</span>\n    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Good job!"</span>)\n<span class="kw">elif</span> score >= <span class="num">70</span>:\n    grade = <span class="str">"C"</span>\n<span class="kw">elif</span> score >= <span class="num">60</span>:\n    grade = <span class="str">"D"</span>\n<span class="kw">else</span>:\n    grade = <span class="str">"F"</span>\n    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"See me after class."</span>)\n\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Score: {score} → Grade: {grade}"</span>)\n<span class="cm"># Output: Good job!</span>\n<span class="cm"># Output: Score: 85 → Grade: B</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Why does order matter?</strong> If we checked <code>score >= 60</code> first, a student with 95 would match that condition and get a "D". Always put the <em>most restrictive</em> condition first. This is a common source of bugs in beginner code.</p>'

+ '<div class="think-box"><div class="think-label">THINK ABOUT IT</div><div class="think-body">What values does Python consider "truthy" vs "falsy"? Beyond explicit Booleans, Python treats <code>0</code>, <code>0.0</code>, <code>""</code>, <code>[]</code>, <code>{}</code>, <code>None</code> as <strong>False</strong>. Everything else is <strong>True</strong>. This means <code>if my_list:</code> is a clean way to check if a list is non-empty — no need for <code>if len(my_list) > 0</code>.</div></div>'

/* --- Ternary Operator --- */
+ '<p class="l-text">Python\'s <strong>ternary (conditional) expression</strong> lets you write simple if/else in a single line. Use it for simple value assignments, not for complex logic:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># Ternary expression: value_if_true if condition else value_if_false</span>\nage = <span class="num">20</span>\nstatus = <span class="str">"adult"</span> <span class="kw">if</span> age >= <span class="num">18</span> <span class="kw">else</span> <span class="str">"minor"</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Nested ternary — AVOID this, it\'s unreadable</span>\nlabel = <span class="str">"senior"</span> <span class="kw">if</span> age >= <span class="num">65</span> <span class="kw">else</span> (<span class="str">"adult"</span> <span class="kw">if</span> age >= <span class="num">18</span> <span class="kw">else</span> <span class="str">"minor"</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># Better: use if/elif/else for 3+ branches</span></code></pre></div>'

/* --- Match-Case --- */
+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Structural Pattern Matching</strong> (Python 3.10+) brings powerful switch-case syntax. Unlike C\'s switch, Python\'s <code>match</code> can destructure data, match types, and use guard clauses:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PSEUDOCODE</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">handle_command</span>(command):\n    <span class="kw">match</span> command.split():\n        <span class="kw">case</span> [<span class="str">"quit"</span>]:\n            <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Goodbye!"</span>)\n        <span class="kw">case</span> [<span class="str">"load"</span>, filename]:\n            <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Loading {filename}..."</span>)\n        <span class="kw">case</span> [<span class="str">"save"</span>, filename, <span class="str">"as"</span>, fmt]:\n            <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Saving {filename} as {fmt}"</span>)\n        <span class="kw">case</span> [<span class="str">"resize"</span>, w, h] <span class="kw">if</span> w.isdigit() <span class="kw">and</span> h.isdigit():\n            <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Resizing to {w}x{h}"</span>)  <span class="cm"># guard clause</span>\n        <span class="kw">case</span> _:\n            <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Unknown command"</span>)\n\n<span class="fn">handle_command</span>(<span class="str">"load model.pkl"</span>)   <span class="cm"># Loading model.pkl...</span>\n<span class="fn">handle_command</span>(<span class="str">"save data as csv"</span>) <span class="cm"># Saving data as csv</span></code></pre></div>'

/* --- Guard Clauses --- */
+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Guard clauses</strong> are a pattern where you handle edge cases early with <code>return</code>, so the main logic isn\'t deeply nested. Compare these two approaches:</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-compare"><div class="compare-col"><div class="compare-label">ANTI-PATTERN: Deep Nesting</div><div class="compare-body"><pre><code><span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">process</span>(data):\n    <span class="kw">if</span> data <span class="kw">is not</span> <span class="kw">None</span>:\n        <span class="kw">if</span> <span class="fn">len</span>(data) > <span class="num">0</span>:\n            <span class="kw">if</span> data[<span class="num">0</span>] != <span class="str">""</span>:\n                <span class="cm"># actual logic</span>\n                <span class="cm"># buried here</span>\n                result = transform(data)\n                <span class="kw">return</span> result\n    <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="kw">None</span></code></pre></div></div><div class="compare-col"><div class="compare-label">CLEAN: Guard Clauses</div><div class="compare-body"><pre><code><span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">process</span>(data):\n    <span class="kw">if</span> data <span class="kw">is</span> <span class="kw">None</span>:\n        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="kw">None</span>\n    <span class="kw">if</span> <span class="fn">len</span>(data) == <span class="num">0</span>:\n        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="kw">None</span>\n    <span class="kw">if</span> data[<span class="num">0</span>] == <span class="str">""</span>:\n        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="kw">None</span>\n\n    <span class="cm"># main logic at top level</span>\n    result = transform(data)\n    <span class="kw">return</span> result</code></pre></div></div></div>'

+ '<div class="l-note"><strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> If you have more than 2 levels of nesting, refactor with guard clauses. Your future self (reading this code at 2 AM debugging a production issue) will thank you.</div>'

