/* ============================================================
   SECTION 2: Loops & Iteration
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">2. Loops & Iteration</h2>'

+ '<div class="calc-highlight"><strong>Core idea:</strong> Loops let you execute a block of code repeatedly. Python has two loop types: <code>for</code> (iterate over a sequence) and <code>while</code> (repeat while a condition is true). The <code>for</code> loop is used ~90% of the time in Python because Python\'s iterator protocol is so powerful.</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>The <code>for</code> loop</strong> iterates over any <em>iterable</em> — lists, strings, ranges, dictionaries, files, generators, and more. Unlike C-style <code>for(i=0; i<n; i++)</code>, Python\'s for loop directly gives you each element:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># Basic for loop with range()</span>\n<span class="kw">for</span> i <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="fn">range</span>(<span class="num">5</span>):\n    <span class="fn">print</span>(i, end=<span class="str">" "</span>)  <span class="cm"># 0 1 2 3 4</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># enumerate() — get index AND value</span>\nfruits = [<span class="str">"apple"</span>, <span class="str">"banana"</span>, <span class="str">"cherry"</span>]\n<span class="kw">for</span> idx, fruit <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="fn">enumerate</span>(fruits):\n    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"{idx}: {fruit}"</span>)\n<span class="cm"># 0: apple   1: banana   2: cherry</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># zip() — iterate multiple sequences in parallel</span>\nnames = [<span class="str">"Alice"</span>, <span class="str">"Bob"</span>, <span class="str">"Charlie"</span>]\nscores = [<span class="num">92</span>, <span class="num">87</span>, <span class="num">78</span>]\n<span class="kw">for</span> name, score <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="fn">zip</span>(names, scores):\n    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"{name}: {score}"</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># Iterating over a dictionary</span>\nconfig = {<span class="str">"lr"</span>: <span class="num">0.001</span>, <span class="str">"epochs"</span>: <span class="num">50</span>, <span class="str">"batch"</span>: <span class="num">32</span>}\n<span class="kw">for</span> key, value <span class="kw">in</span> config.items():\n    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"{key} = {value}"</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># range() with start, stop, step</span>\n<span class="kw">for</span> i <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="fn">range</span>(<span class="num">10</span>, <span class="num">0</span>, -<span class="num">2</span>):\n    <span class="fn">print</span>(i, end=<span class="str">" "</span>)  <span class="cm"># 10 8 6 4 2</span></code></pre></div>'

/* --- Loop Comparison Table --- */
+ '<p class="l-text">Here is a comparison of all the major loop patterns you will use in Python:</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-cards">'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="cc-icon">&#x1F501;</div><div class="cc-title">range(n)</div><div class="cc-body">Generates integers 0 to n-1.<br><code>range(3)</code> → 0, 1, 2<br><code>range(2,8,2)</code> → 2, 4, 6<br>Memory efficient: generates on demand.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="cc-icon">&#x1F522;</div><div class="cc-title">enumerate()</div><div class="cc-body">Adds a counter to any iterable.<br>Returns (index, element) tuples.<br>Avoids manual index tracking.<br>Accepts optional <code>start=</code> param.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="cc-icon">&#x1F500;</div><div class="cc-title">zip()</div><div class="cc-body">Pairs elements from 2+ iterables.<br>Stops at the shortest iterable.<br>Use <code>zip_longest</code> from itertools to pad.<br>Unzip with <code>zip(*pairs)</code>.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="cc-icon">&#x1F504;</div><div class="cc-title">while</div><div class="cc-body">Repeats until condition is False.<br>Risk of infinite loops!<br>Use for unknown iteration count.<br>Common: reading input, convergence.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

/* --- While loop & control --- */
+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>The <code>while</code> loop</strong> repeats as long as a condition holds. Use it when you don\'t know how many iterations you need — for example, reading user input until they type "quit", or training a model until loss converges:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># While loop with convergence check (ML-style)</span>\nloss = <span class="num">10.0</span>\nepoch = <span class="num">0</span>\n<span class="kw">while</span> loss > <span class="num">0.01</span>:\n    loss *= <span class="num">0.85</span>  <span class="cm"># simulate training</span>\n    epoch += <span class="num">1</span>\n    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Epoch {epoch}: loss = {loss:.4f}"</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># break — exit the loop immediately</span>\n<span class="kw">for</span> num <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="fn">range</span>(<span class="num">1000</span>):\n    <span class="kw">if</span> num ** <span class="num">2</span> > <span class="num">500</span>:\n        <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"First n where n^2 > 500: {num}"</span>)\n        <span class="kw">break</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># continue — skip this iteration, go to next</span>\n<span class="kw">for</span> i <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="fn">range</span>(<span class="num">10</span>):\n    <span class="kw">if</span> i % <span class="num">3</span> == <span class="num">0</span>:\n        <span class="kw">continue</span>  <span class="cm"># skip multiples of 3</span>\n    <span class="fn">print</span>(i, end=<span class="str">" "</span>)  <span class="cm"># 1 2 4 5 7 8</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># else on a for loop — runs if loop completes WITHOUT break</span>\n<span class="kw">for</span> n <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="fn">range</span>(<span class="num">2</span>, <span class="num">20</span>):\n    <span class="kw">for</span> d <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="fn">range</span>(<span class="num">2</span>, n):\n        <span class="kw">if</span> n % d == <span class="num">0</span>:\n            <span class="kw">break</span>\n    <span class="kw">else</span>:\n        <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"{n} is prime"</span>)</code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="l-note"><strong>Pro tip:</strong> The <code>for/else</code> pattern is uniquely Pythonic. The <code>else</code> block runs only if the loop completed normally (no <code>break</code>). It is perfect for search operations: the loop searches, <code>break</code> if found, <code>else</code> handles "not found". Most Python developers underuse this feature.</div>'

/* ============================================================
   SECTION 3: Comprehensions
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">3. Comprehensions</h2>'

+ '<div class="calc-highlight"><strong>Core idea:</strong> Comprehensions are Python\'s most elegant feature — they let you build lists, dicts, and sets in a single expression. They are not just syntactic sugar: comprehensions are <em>faster</em> than equivalent for-loops because Python optimizes them internally with specialized bytecode.</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>List comprehension</strong> is the most common form. The syntax is <code>[expression for item in iterable if condition]</code>:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># Basic list comprehension</span>\nsquares = [x**<span class="num">2</span> <span class="kw">for</span> x <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="fn">range</span>(<span class="num">10</span>)]\n<span class="cm"># [0, 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64, 81]</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># With filter condition</span>\neven_squares = [x**<span class="num">2</span> <span class="kw">for</span> x <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="fn">range</span>(<span class="num">10</span>) <span class="kw">if</span> x % <span class="num">2</span> == <span class="num">0</span>]\n<span class="cm"># [0, 4, 16, 36, 64]</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Dict comprehension</span>\nword_lengths = {w: <span class="fn">len</span>(w) <span class="kw">for</span> w <span class="kw">in</span> [<span class="str">"hello"</span>, <span class="str">"world"</span>, <span class="str">"python"</span>]}\n<span class="cm"># {\'hello\': 5, \'world\': 5, \'python\': 6}</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Set comprehension (removes duplicates automatically)</span>\nunique_lengths = {<span class="fn">len</span>(w) <span class="kw">for</span> w <span class="kw">in</span> [<span class="str">"hi"</span>, <span class="str">"no"</span>, <span class="str">"yes"</span>, <span class="str">"ok"</span>]}\n<span class="cm"># {2, 3}</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Generator expression (lazy — yields items one at a time)</span>\ntotal = <span class="fn">sum</span>(x**<span class="num">2</span> <span class="kw">for</span> x <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="fn">range</span>(<span class="num">1000000</span>))  <span class="cm"># no list in memory!</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Nested comprehension — flattening a 2D list</span>\nmatrix = [[<span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">2</span>, <span class="num">3</span>], [<span class="num">4</span>, <span class="num">5</span>, <span class="num">6</span>], [<span class="num">7</span>, <span class="num">8</span>, <span class="num">9</span>]]\nflat = [num <span class="kw">for</span> row <span class="kw">in</span> matrix <span class="kw">for</span> num <span class="kw">in</span> row]\n<span class="cm"># [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9]</span></code></pre></div>'

/* --- Side by side comparison --- */
+ '<p class="l-text">To understand comprehensions, see them next to their for-loop equivalents:</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-compare"><div class="compare-col"><div class="compare-label">FOR LOOP</div><div class="compare-body"><pre><code>result = []\n<span class="kw">for</span> x <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="fn">range</span>(<span class="num">10</span>):\n    <span class="kw">if</span> x % <span class="num">2</span> == <span class="num">0</span>:\n        result.append(x ** <span class="num">2</span>)\n<span class="cm"># 4 lines, creates list manually</span></code></pre></div></div><div class="compare-col"><div class="compare-label">COMPREHENSION</div><div class="compare-body"><pre><code>result = [x**<span class="num">2</span> <span class="kw">for</span> x <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="fn">range</span>(<span class="num">10</span>)\n          <span class="kw">if</span> x % <span class="num">2</span> == <span class="num">0</span>]\n\n<span class="cm"># 1 expression, ~30% faster</span></code></pre></div></div></div>'

+ '<div class="think-box"><div class="think-label">THINK ABOUT IT</div><div class="think-body"><strong>When NOT to use comprehensions:</strong> If your logic requires multiple statements, side effects (like printing), or exceeds ~80 characters per line, use a regular for-loop. Readability is more important than cleverness. A comprehension that takes 3 minutes to understand is worse than a 5-line loop that takes 5 seconds.</div></div>'

