/* ============================================================
   SECTION 6: Lambda & Functional Tools
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">6. Lambda & Functional Tools</h2>'

+ '<div class="calc-highlight"><strong>Core idea:</strong> Python supports functional programming alongside object-oriented programming. Lambda functions are anonymous one-liner functions. Combined with <code>map()</code>, <code>filter()</code>, <code>sorted()</code>, and <code>functools</code>, they enable concise data transformation pipelines. However, know when <strong>not</strong> to use them — readability always wins.</div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># Lambda: anonymous single-expression function</span>\nsquare = <span class="kw">lambda</span> x: x ** <span class="num">2</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="fn">square</span>(<span class="num">5</span>))  <span class="cm"># 25</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># sorted() with key — the #1 use case for lambda</span>\nstudents = [(<span class="str">"Alice"</span>, <span class="num">92</span>), (<span class="str">"Bob"</span>, <span class="num">87</span>), (<span class="str">"Charlie"</span>, <span class="num">95</span>)]\nby_score = <span class="fn">sorted</span>(students, key=<span class="kw">lambda</span> s: s[<span class="num">1</span>], reverse=<span class="kw">True</span>)\n<span class="cm"># [(\'Charlie\', 95), (\'Alice\', 92), (\'Bob\', 87)]</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># map() — apply function to every element</span>\nnumbers = [<span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">2</span>, <span class="num">3</span>, <span class="num">4</span>, <span class="num">5</span>]\ndoubled = <span class="fn">list</span>(<span class="fn">map</span>(<span class="kw">lambda</span> x: x * <span class="num">2</span>, numbers))\n<span class="cm"># [2, 4, 6, 8, 10]</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># filter() — keep elements where function returns True</span>\nevens = <span class="fn">list</span>(<span class="fn">filter</span>(<span class="kw">lambda</span> x: x % <span class="num">2</span> == <span class="num">0</span>, numbers))\n<span class="cm"># [2, 4]</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># functools.reduce() — accumulate values</span>\n<span class="kw">from</span> functools <span class="kw">import</span> reduce\nproduct = <span class="fn">reduce</span>(<span class="kw">lambda</span> a, b: a * b, [<span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">2</span>, <span class="num">3</span>, <span class="num">4</span>])\n<span class="cm"># 24  (1*2=2, 2*3=6, 6*4=24)</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># functools.partial() — freeze some arguments</span>\n<span class="kw">from</span> functools <span class="kw">import</span> partial\n\n<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">power</span>(base, exponent):\n    <span class="kw">return</span> base ** exponent\n\nsquare = <span class="fn">partial</span>(power, exponent=<span class="num">2</span>)\ncube = <span class="fn">partial</span>(power, exponent=<span class="num">3</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="fn">square</span>(<span class="num">5</span>), <span class="fn">cube</span>(<span class="num">5</span>))  <span class="cm"># 25 125</span></code></pre></div>'

/* --- Lambda vs Def Comparison --- */
+ '<p class="l-text">When should you use lambda vs a named function? Here is the decision framework:</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-compare"><div class="compare-col"><div class="compare-label">USE LAMBDA WHEN</div><div class="compare-body"><ul class="l-list"><li>Simple, single expression</li><li>Passing to <code>sorted(key=)</code>, <code>map()</code>, <code>filter()</code></li><li>One-time use, no reuse needed</li><li>Logic fits in ~40 characters</li></ul><pre><code><span class="cm"># Good lambda usage</span>\nwords.sort(key=<span class="kw">lambda</span> w: <span class="fn">len</span>(w))\n<span class="fn">max</span>(data, key=<span class="kw">lambda</span> x: x[<span class="str">"score"</span>])</code></pre></div></div><div class="compare-col"><div class="compare-label">USE DEF WHEN</div><div class="compare-body"><ul class="l-list"><li>Multiple statements needed</li><li>Complex logic or branching</li><li>Reused in multiple places</li><li>Needs a docstring</li><li>Error handling required</li></ul><pre><code><span class="cm"># Better as named function</span>\n<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">normalize</span>(text):\n    text = text.lower().strip()\n    text = re.sub(<span class="str">r"\\s+"</span>, <span class="str">" "</span>, text)\n    <span class="kw">return</span> text</code></pre></div></div></div>'

/* --- Operator module --- */
+ '<p class="l-text">The <code>operator</code> module provides function equivalents of Python\'s operators. These are faster than lambdas because they avoid the overhead of creating a new function object:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">from</span> operator <span class="kw">import</span> itemgetter, attrgetter, mul\n<span class="kw">from</span> functools <span class="kw">import</span> reduce\n\n<span class="cm"># Instead of lambda x: x[1]</span>\nstudents = [(<span class="str">"Alice"</span>, <span class="num">92</span>), (<span class="str">"Bob"</span>, <span class="num">87</span>), (<span class="str">"Charlie"</span>, <span class="num">95</span>)]\nby_score = <span class="fn">sorted</span>(students, key=<span class="fn">itemgetter</span>(<span class="num">1</span>))\n\n<span class="cm"># Instead of lambda a, b: a * b</span>\nfactorial_10 = <span class="fn">reduce</span>(mul, <span class="fn">range</span>(<span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">11</span>))\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"10! = {factorial_10}"</span>)  <span class="cm"># 3628800</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Multi-key sorting</span>\nrecords = [(<span class="str">"Math"</span>, <span class="num">85</span>), (<span class="str">"CS"</span>, <span class="num">92</span>), (<span class="str">"Math"</span>, <span class="num">91</span>), (<span class="str">"CS"</span>, <span class="num">88</span>)]\nby_dept_score = <span class="fn">sorted</span>(records, key=<span class="fn">itemgetter</span>(<span class="num">0</span>, <span class="num">1</span>))\n<span class="cm"># [(\'CS\', 88), (\'CS\', 92), (\'Math\', 85), (\'Math\', 91)]</span></code></pre></div>'

/* --- Plotly: Functional Programming Pipeline --- */
+ '<div class="calc-graph"><div class="cg-title">Functional Pipeline: Data Transformation Flow</div></div>'
