/* ============================================================
   SECTION 7: Exception Handling
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">7. Exception Handling</h2>'

+ '<div class="calc-highlight"><strong>Core idea:</strong> Real programs deal with bad input, missing files, network failures, and edge cases. Exceptions are Python\'s mechanism for separating <em>normal flow</em> from <em>error flow</em>. With <code>try/except/else/finally</code> you catch problems precisely, recover gracefully, and guarantee cleanup — instead of crashing or silently producing wrong results.</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text">An exception is an object raised when something goes wrong. If nothing catches it, Python unwinds the call stack and prints a traceback. The basic skeleton has four optional clauses: <code>try</code> wraps the risky code, <code>except</code> handles specific error types, <code>else</code> runs only when no exception fired, and <code>finally</code> always runs (cleanup).</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># try / except / else / finally — the full skeleton</span>\n<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">safe_divide</span>(a, b):\n    <span class="kw">try</span>:\n        result = a / b\n    <span class="kw">except</span> ZeroDivisionError:\n        <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Cannot divide by zero"</span>)\n        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="kw">None</span>\n    <span class="kw">except</span> TypeError <span class="kw">as</span> e:\n        <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Type error: {e}"</span>)\n        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="kw">None</span>\n    <span class="kw">else</span>:\n        <span class="cm"># Only runs if NO exception occurred</span>\n        <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Division succeeded: {result}"</span>)\n        <span class="kw">return</span> result\n    <span class="kw">finally</span>:\n        <span class="cm"># ALWAYS runs — success, exception, or return</span>\n        <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"safe_divide finished"</span>)\n\n<span class="fn">safe_divide</span>(<span class="num">10</span>, <span class="num">2</span>)   <span class="cm"># Division succeeded: 5.0 ; safe_divide finished</span>\n<span class="fn">safe_divide</span>(<span class="num">10</span>, <span class="num">0</span>)   <span class="cm"># Cannot divide by zero ; safe_divide finished</span>\n<span class="fn">safe_divide</span>(<span class="num">10</span>, <span class="str">"x"</span>) <span class="cm"># Type error: ... ; safe_divide finished</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>OUTPUT</span></div><pre class="code-block"><code>Division succeeded: 5.0\nsafe_divide finished\nCannot divide by zero\nsafe_divide finished\nType error: unsupported operand type(s) for /: \'int\' and \'str\'\nsafe_divide finished</code></pre></div>'

/* --- Specific exception types --- */
+ '<p class="l-text">Python has a deep <strong>exception hierarchy</strong>. <code>BaseException</code> is the root; almost everything you catch inherits from <code>Exception</code>. Catching <em>specific</em> types is critical — a bare <code>except:</code> swallows <code>KeyboardInterrupt</code> and <code>SystemExit</code> too, hiding real bugs.</p>'

+ '<div class="l-highlight"><strong>Common built-in exceptions:</strong><ul class="l-list"><li><code>ValueError</code> — right type, wrong value (e.g. <code>int("abc")</code>)</li><li><code>TypeError</code> — wrong type (e.g. <code>len(42)</code>)</li><li><code>KeyError</code> — missing dict key</li><li><code>IndexError</code> — list index out of range</li><li><code>AttributeError</code> — object has no such attribute</li><li><code>FileNotFoundError</code> — file does not exist (subclass of <code>OSError</code>)</li><li><code>StopIteration</code> — iterator exhausted</li><li><code>RuntimeError</code> — generic runtime failure</li></ul></div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># Catch multiple specific types — most specific first</span>\n<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">load_user_age</span>(data, key):\n    <span class="kw">try</span>:\n        raw = data[key]            <span class="cm"># may raise KeyError</span>\n        age = <span class="fn">int</span>(raw)            <span class="cm"># may raise ValueError</span>\n        <span class="kw">if</span> age &lt; <span class="num">0</span>:\n            <span class="kw">raise</span> <span class="fn">ValueError</span>(<span class="str">f"Negative age: {age}"</span>)\n        <span class="kw">return</span> age\n    <span class="kw">except</span> KeyError:\n        <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Missing key: {key!r}"</span>)\n    <span class="kw">except</span> ValueError <span class="kw">as</span> e:\n        <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Invalid age value: {e}"</span>)\n    <span class="kw">except</span> (TypeError, AttributeError) <span class="kw">as</span> e:\n        <span class="cm"># Catch multiple related types in one clause</span>\n        <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Bad input shape: {e}"</span>)\n    <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="kw">None</span>\n\n<span class="fn">load_user_age</span>({<span class="str">"age"</span>: <span class="str">"30"</span>}, <span class="str">"age"</span>)    <span class="cm"># 30</span>\n<span class="fn">load_user_age</span>({}, <span class="str">"age"</span>)              <span class="cm"># Missing key: \'age\'</span>\n<span class="fn">load_user_age</span>({<span class="str">"age"</span>: <span class="str">"abc"</span>}, <span class="str">"age"</span>)   <span class="cm"># Invalid age value: ...</span>\n<span class="fn">load_user_age</span>({<span class="str">"age"</span>: <span class="str">"-5"</span>}, <span class="str">"age"</span>)    <span class="cm"># Invalid age value: Negative age: -5</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<p class="l-text">Working with files is the textbook case for <code>FileNotFoundError</code>:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> json\n\n<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">load_config</span>(path):\n    <span class="kw">try</span>:\n        <span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">open</span>(path, <span class="str">"r"</span>, encoding=<span class="str">"utf-8"</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> f:\n            <span class="kw">return</span> json.<span class="fn">load</span>(f)\n    <span class="kw">except</span> FileNotFoundError:\n        <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Config not found at {path}, using defaults"</span>)\n        <span class="kw">return</span> {<span class="str">"debug"</span>: <span class="kw">False</span>, <span class="str">"workers"</span>: <span class="num">4</span>}\n    <span class="kw">except</span> json.JSONDecodeError <span class="kw">as</span> e:\n        <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Corrupt config at line {e.lineno}: {e.msg}"</span>)\n        <span class="kw">raise</span>   <span class="cm"># re-raise: this one is fatal</span>\n    <span class="kw">except</span> PermissionError:\n        <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"No permission to read {path}"</span>)\n        <span class="kw">raise</span></code></pre></div>'

/* --- Custom exception classes --- */
+ '<p class="l-text">When built-in types do not describe your domain precisely, define <strong>custom exception classes</strong>. They make <code>except</code> clauses self-documenting and let callers catch <em>your</em> errors without catching unrelated ones.</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># Always inherit from Exception (NOT BaseException)</span>\n<span class="kw">class</span> <span class="ty">DatasetError</span>(<span class="ty">Exception</span>):\n    <span class="str">"""Base class for dataset-related errors."""</span>\n\n<span class="kw">class</span> <span class="ty">EmptyDatasetError</span>(<span class="ty">DatasetError</span>):\n    <span class="str">"""Raised when a dataset has zero rows."""</span>\n\n<span class="kw">class</span> <span class="ty">SchemaMismatchError</span>(<span class="ty">DatasetError</span>):\n    <span class="str">"""Raised when columns don\'t match expected schema."""</span>\n    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">__init__</span>(self, expected, got):\n        self.expected = expected\n        self.got = got\n        <span class="fn">super</span>().<span class="fn">__init__</span>(\n            <span class="str">f"Expected columns {expected}, got {got}"</span>\n        )\n\n<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">validate</span>(rows, columns):\n    <span class="kw">if</span> <span class="fn">len</span>(rows) == <span class="num">0</span>:\n        <span class="kw">raise</span> <span class="fn">EmptyDatasetError</span>(<span class="str">"Got 0 rows"</span>)\n    expected = {<span class="str">"id"</span>, <span class="str">"value"</span>}\n    <span class="kw">if</span> <span class="fn">set</span>(columns) != expected:\n        <span class="kw">raise</span> <span class="fn">SchemaMismatchError</span>(expected, <span class="fn">set</span>(columns))\n    <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="kw">True</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Callers can catch the whole family or specific subclasses</span>\n<span class="kw">try</span>:\n    <span class="fn">validate</span>([], [<span class="str">"id"</span>, <span class="str">"value"</span>])\n<span class="kw">except</span> EmptyDatasetError:\n    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Skipping empty file"</span>)\n<span class="kw">except</span> DatasetError <span class="kw">as</span> e:\n    <span class="cm"># Catches SchemaMismatchError and any future DatasetError subclass</span>\n    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Dataset problem: {e}"</span>)</code></pre></div>'

+ '<p class="l-text">Use <code>raise ... from ...</code> to chain exceptions and preserve the original cause — invaluable for debugging:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">class</span> <span class="ty">ModelLoadError</span>(<span class="ty">Exception</span>): <span class="kw">pass</span>\n\n<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">load_model</span>(path):\n    <span class="kw">try</span>:\n        <span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">open</span>(path, <span class="str">"rb"</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> f:\n            <span class="kw">return</span> pickle.<span class="fn">load</span>(f)\n    <span class="kw">except</span> (FileNotFoundError, pickle.UnpicklingError) <span class="kw">as</span> e:\n        <span class="cm"># Wrap low-level errors in a domain-specific one</span>\n        <span class="kw">raise</span> <span class="fn">ModelLoadError</span>(<span class="str">f"Could not load {path}"</span>) <span class="kw">from</span> e</code></pre></div>'

/* --- Context managers (with) --- */
+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Bonus — context managers.</strong> The <code>with</code> statement is a structured way to guarantee setup/teardown, replacing many <code>try/finally</code> blocks. Any object implementing <code>__enter__</code> and <code>__exit__</code> is a context manager. Files, locks, database connections, and temporary directories all use this pattern.</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># Classic: file is closed even if an exception fires</span>\n<span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">open</span>(<span class="str">"data.csv"</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> f:\n    text = f.<span class="fn">read</span>()\n<span class="cm"># f is guaranteed closed here</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Multiple managers in one statement</span>\n<span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">open</span>(<span class="str">"in.txt"</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> src, <span class="fn">open</span>(<span class="str">"out.txt"</span>, <span class="str">"w"</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> dst:\n    dst.<span class="fn">write</span>(src.<span class="fn">read</span>().upper())\n\n<span class="cm"># Build your own with contextlib.contextmanager</span>\n<span class="kw">from</span> contextlib <span class="kw">import</span> contextmanager\n<span class="kw">import</span> time\n\n<span class="pp">@contextmanager</span>\n<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">timer</span>(label):\n    start = time.<span class="fn">perf_counter</span>()\n    <span class="kw">try</span>:\n        <span class="kw">yield</span>                <span class="cm"># control returns to the with-block</span>\n    <span class="kw">finally</span>:\n        elapsed = time.<span class="fn">perf_counter</span>() - start\n        <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"{label}: {elapsed:.3f}s"</span>)\n\n<span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">timer</span>(<span class="str">"train"</span>):\n    model.<span class="fn">fit</span>(X, y)\n<span class="cm"># Prints "train: 1.234s" even if fit() raises</span></code></pre></div>'

/* --- Best practices --- */
+ '<p class="l-text">A few habits separate brittle scripts from production-grade Python:</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-compare"><div class="compare-col"><div class="compare-label">DO</div><div class="compare-body"><ul class="l-list"><li>Catch the <strong>narrowest</strong> exception that makes sense</li><li>Use <code>logging.exception()</code> inside <code>except</code> to capture the traceback</li><li><code>raise NewError(...) from original</code> to keep the cause</li><li>Use custom exception hierarchies for your domain</li><li>Use <code>with</code> for any resource that needs cleanup</li><li>Validate input early and <code>raise ValueError</code> with a clear message</li></ul></div></div><div class="compare-col"><div class="compare-label">DO NOT</div><div class="compare-body"><ul class="l-list"><li>Write bare <code>except:</code> — it hides <code>KeyboardInterrupt</code> and bugs</li><li>Catch <code>Exception</code> just to <code>print</code> and swallow it</li><li>Use exceptions for normal control flow (e.g. loop termination)</li><li>Inherit custom errors from <code>BaseException</code></li><li>Lose the original traceback by rewrapping without <code>from</code></li><li><code>pass</code> in an <code>except</code> block without a comment explaining why</li></ul></div></div></div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> logging\nlog = logging.<span class="fn">getLogger</span>(__name__)\n\n<span class="cm"># Good: narrow, logged, re-raised</span>\n<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">process</span>(record):\n    <span class="kw">try</span>:\n        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="fn">transform</span>(record)\n    <span class="kw">except</span> ValueError:\n        log.<span class="fn">exception</span>(<span class="str">"Bad record %r"</span>, record)\n        <span class="kw">raise</span>          <span class="cm"># let the caller decide</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Bad: hides every problem, you will spend hours debugging this</span>\n<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">process_bad</span>(record):\n    <span class="kw">try</span>:\n        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="fn">transform</span>(record)\n    <span class="kw">except</span>:                <span class="cm"># bare except — never do this</span>\n        <span class="kw">pass</span>             <span class="cm"># silent failure</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="l-note"><strong>Mental model:</strong> Exceptions are not errors — they are <em>signals</em>. <code>StopIteration</code> ends a loop, <code>KeyError</code> tells you a key is missing, <code>FileNotFoundError</code> reports a missing path. Your job is to decide which signals to handle locally, which to let propagate, and which to translate into a more meaningful error for the caller.</div>'

/* --- Summary --- */
+ '<div class="calc-highlight"><strong>Lesson Summary:</strong> You have learned the complete control flow and functions toolkit: conditionals (<code>if/elif/else</code>, match-case, guard clauses), loops (<code>for</code>, <code>while</code>, <code>break/continue/else</code>), comprehensions (list, dict, set, generator), functions (parameters, *args/**kwargs, type hints), scope (LEGB rule, closures), functional tools (lambda, map, filter, reduce, partial), and exception handling (<code>try/except/else/finally</code>, custom exception classes, context managers). These are the patterns you will use in every Python project, from data analysis scripts to full ML pipelines.</div>'

+ '<div class="l-note"><strong>Next lesson:</strong> Data Structures Deep Dive — lists, tuples, dictionaries, sets, and when to use each. We will explore time complexity, hash tables, and data structure patterns that are critical for efficient algorithms and clean code.</div>'


